1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:03,420
World War Two ended in 1945.

2
00:00:03,970 --> 00:00:07,170
But for some Japanese soldiers,
it went on for decades.

3
00:00:07,720 --> 00:00:14,440
They hid in the jungles of Pacific islands, not knowing
that Japan had surrendered long ago. Or refusing to believe it.

4
00:00:14,990 --> 00:00:18,510
Planes dropped leaflets over the forests:
"The war is over."

5
00:00:19,060 --> 00:00:24,160
But the soldiers were sure it was an enemy trick.
They had been taught that surrender was worse than death.

6
00:00:24,710 --> 00:00:30,020
On the island of Guam, Sergeant Shoichi Yokoi
lived for 28 years in a hole he dug himself.

7
00:00:30,570 --> 00:00:37,290
He caught fish, wove clothes from tree bark,
and was afraid to come out. He was found only in 1972.

8
00:00:37,840 --> 00:00:43,520
On the Philippine island of Lubang, Lieutenant Hiroo Onoda
and three comrades kept fighting a guerrilla war.

9
00:00:44,070 --> 00:00:49,210
Year after year, his companions were killed or surrendered.
Onoda was the last one left.

10
00:00:49,760 --> 00:00:57,650
He refused to lay down his arms, until in 1974, his former commander
flew into the jungle and personally cancelled the order.

11
00:00:58,200 --> 00:01:02,830
And a few months later, on the island of Morotai,
they found Private Teruo Nakamura.

12
00:01:03,380 --> 00:01:08,370
He was the last soldier to walk out of the jungle,
almost thirty years after the war had ended.
